<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Honduras</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Honduras</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Honduras is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>. Before Spanish colonisation in the 16th century its territory held several Mesoamerican cultures, most notably the Maya, whose state at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copan">Copán</a> declined in the 9th century. Honduras became independent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1821, was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mexican-empire">First Mexican Empire</a> until 1823, and then of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">United Provinces of Central America</a>. It has been an independent republic with elections since 1838. In the early 20th century transnational agricultural companies dominated it, and the term &quot;banana republic&quot; was first coined for its politics. In 1960 the northern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosquito-coast">Mosquito Coast</a> was transferred from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> to Honduras.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It joined five states, Guatemala, El Salvador, <b>Honduras</b>, Nicaragua and Costa Rica, under a federal government seated in Guatemala City.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Diego de Landa, one son of the Cocom ruler survived the massacre of his family at Mayapan only because he was away trading in <b>Honduras</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/league-of-mayapan">League of Mayapan · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Most Salvadorans were landless or land-poor labourers and smallholders, and population pressure drove large-scale migration to <b>Honduras</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Carrera’s armies intervened repeatedly in El Salvador and <b>Honduras</b>, defeating the liberal coalition at La Arada in 1851 and taking San Salvador in 1863, and Guatemalan contingents joined the Central American war against William Walker in 1856–1857.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1911 the banana magnate Samuel Zemurray financed an invasion of <b>Honduras</b> that restored the former president Manuel Bonilla, who rewarded him with land concessions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Today it is held by the extant Maya peoples of Guatemala, Belize, western <b>Honduras</b> and the Mexican states of Tabasco, Chiapas, Quintana Roo, Campeche and Yucatán.</blockquote><a href="/religion/maya-religion">Maya religion · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
